Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: “Global Material Adverse Effect” means any change, event, violation, inaccuracy, effect, or circumstance (each, an “Effect”) that, individually or in combination with other Effects, (a) has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (b) would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay, or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided, however, that the following shall not be considered in determining whether a Global Material Adverse Effect has occurred: (i) changes in general economic or political conditions globally, or changes in conditions in the financial or capital markets globally, including changes in interest rates or currency exchange rates; (ii) changes in industry conditions affecting the Company and its Subsidiaries, provided such changes do not disproportionately affect the Company compared to other industry participants; (iii) any acts of war, terrorism, or natural disasters, unless the Company is disproportionately affected relative to other companies in the same industry; (iv) any failure by the Company to meet any financial projections or forecasts, unless such failure results from a change or event that is not otherwise excluded by this definition; (v) any change resulting from compliance with this Agreement or from any action taken with the express consent of the Buyer; (vi) any change in applicable laws or regulations, unless such change disproportionately affects the Company. Effects described in clauses (i) through (vi) shall only be considered if they disproportionately impact the Company relative to other similar entities. (Pages 14-16)\nAnswer:
Choices:

