Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: “Company Material Adverse Effect” means any change, event, violation, inaccuracy, effect or circumstance (each, an “Effect”) that, individually or taken together with all other Effects that exist or have occurred prior to the date of determination of the occurrence of the Company Material Adverse Effect, (a) has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole or (b) would, or would reasonably be expected to, prevent, materially delay or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided that, in the case of clause (a) only, none of the following (by itself or when aggregated) to the extent occurring after the date of this Agreement will be deemed to be or constitute a Company Material Adverse Effect or will be taken into account when determining whether a Company Material Adverse Effect has occurred or may, would or could occur: (i) changes in global economic conditions, including recessions or depressions; (ii) changes in conditions in the financial markets, credit markets or capital markets, including (A) changes in interest rates or credit ratings; (B) changes in exchange rates for the currencies of any country; or (C) any suspension of trading in securities generally on any securities exchange; (iii) general changes in conditions in the industries in which the Company and its Subsidiaries conduct business; (iv) regulatory, legislative or political changes, including new laws or changes in the interpretation of existing laws; (v) any geopolitical conditions, acts of war, sabotage, terrorism or military actions, including any escalation or general worsening of any such conditions; (vi) natural disasters, pandemics or contagious disease outbreaks, including COVID-19, and any governmental responses to such events; (vii) the public announcement or pendency of this Agreement or the Merger, except where specifically related to the Company Material Adverse Effect; (viii) actions taken or refrained from being taken with the express written consent of the Parent; (ix) changes in GAAP or other accounting standards; (x) changes in the price or trading volume of the Company Common Stock or Indebtedness; (xi) failure to meet internal or external projections, forecasts, or estimates of revenues, earnings, or other financial performance, unless due to underlying causes not otherwise excluded; and (xii) Transaction Litigation; provided further, that with respect to clauses (i) through (vi) and (ix), such Effects shall be taken into account in determining whether a “Company Material Adverse Effect” has occurred only if they disproportionately affect the Company relative to other companies in the same industry. (Pages 12-14)
